<p>What phase changes will take place when water is subjected to varying pressure at a constant temperature of 0.005 &deg;C? At 40 &deg;C? At &minus;40 &deg;C?</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/10.E:_Liquids_and_Solids_(Exercises)" class="mt-section"><span id="S10.4.2"></span><h3 id="S10.4.2-42535">S10.4.2 </h3>

<p>At low pressures and 0.005 &deg;C, the water is a gas. As the pressure increases to 4.6 torr, the water becomes a solid; as the pressure increases still more, it becomes a liquid. At 40 &deg;C, water at low pressure is a vapor; at pressures higher than about 75 torr, it converts into a liquid. At &minus;40 &deg;C, water goes from a gas to a solid as the pressure increases above very low values.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/10.E:_Liquids_and_Solids_(Exercises)" class="mt-section"><span id="Q10.4.3"></span><h3 id="Q10.4.3-42535">Q10.4.3</h3>

<p>Pressure cookers allow food to cook faster because the higher pressure inside the pressure cooker increases the boiling temperature of water. A particular pressure cooker has a safety valve that is set to vent steam if the pressure exceeds 3.4 atm. What is the approximate maximum temperature that can be reached inside this pressure cooker? Explain your reasoning.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/10.E:_Liquids_and_Solids_(Exercises)" class="mt-section"><span id="Q10.4.4"></span><h3 id="Q10.4.4-42535">Q10.4.4</h3>

<p>From the phase diagram for carbon dioxide, determine the state of CO<sub>2</sub> at:</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: lower-alpha;">
    <li>20 &deg;C and 1000 kPa</li>
    <li>10 &deg;C and 2000 kPa</li>
    <li>10 &deg;C and 100 kPa</li>
    <li>&minus;40 &deg;C and 500 kPa</li>
    <li>&minus;80 &deg;C and 1500 kPa</li>
    <li>&minus;80 &deg;C and 10 kPa</li>
