<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/10.E:_Liquids_and_Solids_(Exercises)" class="mt-section"><span id="Q10.5.1"></span><h3 id="Q10.5.1-42535">Q10.5.1</h3>


<p>What types of liquids typically form amorphous solids?</p>


</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/10.E:_Liquids_and_Solids_(Exercises)" class="mt-section"><span id="S10.5.1"></span><h3 id="S10.5.1-42535">S10.5.1 </h3>



<p>Amorphous solids lack an ordered internal structure. Liquid materials that contain large, cumbersome molecules that cannot move readily into ordered positions generally form such solids.</p>



